WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.680
A free browser tool now hides your real name and address from ai chats before they even leave your device. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 172. You know that feeling when you tap Send, then Freeze because your address was still sitting in the box? A free browser addon called AgentClogue ai is built to stop that exact moment. A browser addon is a tiny extra tool you clip onto Chrome, like a charm you snap onto a backpack.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.400
Inside, it lives a small artificial intelligence model named Rampart from National Design Studio.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:43.200
Artificial Intelligence or AI here just means software that has learned patterns from lots of examples.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:49.160
Rampart is only 14.7 megabytes and it runs completely inside your browser.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.420
It scans what you typed and looks for names, addresses, and other private details.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.820
details. Before your message is sent, those details get swapped for realistic

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.080
stand-ins. After the chat replies, the real details are put back into the answer

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.860
you see. Picture covering names on a school permission slip with stickers before

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.240
the copier runs. Then, picture peeling those stickers off when the copy comes

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:18.240
home, so you still have a useful page. The stand-ins work like a movie extra

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.260
wearing a fake name tag, so the scene still makes sense. The add-on works with

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:30.060
chat GPT, Claude, Gemini, Grock, and DeepSeek, the popular AI chat tools people already use.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.860
It is completely free to install. Most of us use those chats for school projects, creative

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:41.360
writing, or quick questions, and personal stuff slips in without a second thought. That leftover

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.900
personal stuff could be stored or misused later. Manually stripping private details out of

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.700
every prompt is a ridiculous chore to keep doing by hand. Search for the AgentClote AI extension

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.840
in the Chrome Web Store, add it, and type a prompt that includes a made-up name or address.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:02.900
Watch the swap happen by itself. Here's the part that still surprises people,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.680
even after they install a helper like that. Your phone can suggest the next word while you text,

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.520
and that suggestion never has to travel to a far away computer. A small privacy model can live

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:20.700
the same way, right inside the browser tab you already have open. Step 1 is simple. You type

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.280
Step a sentence the way you always do.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.140
Step two, the little model scans that sentence for patterns.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.940
The way a lunch line checker glances at backpacks without unpacking every binder.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.160
It is looking for number clusters that look like phone numbers, and for word shapes that

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.920
look like names or street addresses.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:44.500
Step three, it swaps the real bits for stand-ins that still sound like a normal sentence, so the

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.260
distant chat can answer without seeing the originals.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:52.620
Step 4. When the answer comes home, the real bits pop back in on your screen.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.360
Because the model is tiny, that scan finishes in a split second and does not make the page

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:04.260
feel sluggish. The scanning step itself needs no internet connection, like a pocket calculator

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.640
that never calls the school's giant computer lab. A full pass stays on your device, which

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:15.100
is why a made up address in a test prompt is such a good first experiment. Once you have

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.240
seen that local swap, you have the mechanism, not just the product name.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:25.480
If you want something playful after that, Fable AI can build an entire game from one simple

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:25.880
request.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.740
You tell it you want a graphically beautiful game about zooming out with surprising

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.480
reveals, and it builds something you can play instantly in your browser.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:40.320
That is like handing a friend a single sticky note that says, make me a world, then watching

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.500
them unfold a map you can walk around in.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:49.060
You do not need coding or design skills, which is the whole point if you like games, art, or just messing around.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.660
Open Fable AI, type that zoom out request and start playing.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.880
Notice how the world changes as you zoom out, and watch what unexpected things appear.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.440
The way a Minecraft map keeps adding land when you keep walking.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:09.460
The same question asked to another AI called Astra produced slick results, but with less simulated curiosity.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:13.400
Fables seemed more interested in the outcomes it generated.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.840
There is also a new article written for people who have never studied machine learning,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.000
but want to know what chat GPT actually does.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.140
Machine learning just means teaching software by showing it many examples, instead of writing

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:28.560
every rule by hand.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.860
TechnoSports published the piece in plain language, without assuming you already know technical terms.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.940
It walks through the basics of how an AI can answer questions or write stories.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.820
Read the first few sections, then pause after each part and explain it out loud to yourself

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.880
or someone nearby, like teaching a chapter back to a study partner.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.980
That out loud pause is the experiment, not a test.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:00.560
A recent study found that when AI chatbots answer in a friendly, conversational tone, people

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.320
are more likely to trust incorrect health information.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:08.520
Studies finds reports that the style of the reply changes how believable the content

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.140
feels, even when the content is wrong.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.900
Double check anything medical with a real doctor or a trusted source, the way you would not

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.900
swallow a confident, wrong answer just because a teacher said it warmly.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:27.640
Meta's Muse AI assistant can read your messages, calendar, and notes on your Mac, which makes

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.400
it powerful and also a little close to the bone.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.100
The Verge notes that every new AI tool comes with a choice about what data you are okay

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.920
sharing, like deciding whether a new app gets a spare house key.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:45.560
For all its smarts, Muse does not actually know how to describe itself in simple terms.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:50.440
Looking back over the week we just finished, a few big threads kept returning.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:56.040
Google showed an AI that notices a friend's birthday and suggests a gift drawn from your

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.400
own photos and calendar.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:03.580
Open AI started tracking when its AI slips up, a change that could help keep people safer.

